## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to accumulate using wider accumulators? 
- What is the performance impact on platforms with native popcount intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

